1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,388
In 1902,

2
00:00:01,388 --> 00:00:02,421
people in Hanoi were cutting

3
00:00:02,421 --> 00:00:04,221
the tails off live rats...

4
00:00:04,221 --> 00:00:05,800
and letting them go.

5
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,115
Because the city was paying for tails.

6
00:00:08,365 --> 00:00:11,372
This video is about one strange rule.

7
00:00:11,372 --> 00:00:13,903
Every time someone pays for a number...

8
00:00:13,903 --> 00:00:15,180
people find the cheapest

9
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,640
way to make the number.

10
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:19,839
Not the thing you wanted. Just the number.

11
00:00:19,839 --> 00:00:21,000
We'll see it with rats,

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,579
with vacuum cleaners,

13
00:00:22,579 --> 00:00:24,077
with chocolate pudding,

14
00:00:24,077 --> 00:00:26,085
with one of the biggest banks in America,

15
00:00:26,085 --> 00:00:28,663
and with the traffic of a whole city.

16
00:00:28,663 --> 00:00:31,507
And once — with emus —

17
00:00:31,507 --> 00:00:33,918
where paying people actually worked.

18
00:00:37,083 --> 00:00:41,100
Hanoi, French colonial Vietnam.

19
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,760
The French built new sewers

20
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:44,978
under their part of town.

21
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:46,638
Rats loved them.

22
00:00:46,638 --> 00:00:47,996
They came up through the toilets,

23
00:00:47,996 --> 00:00:50,802
and plague was spreading across Asia.

24
00:00:51,052 --> 00:00:53,119
So in April 1902 the city

25
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:54,953
opened a hunt for everyone:

26
00:00:54,953 --> 00:00:57,252
bring in a rat tail, get paid —

27
00:00:57,252 --> 00:01:01,582
reportedly one cent a tail. Just the tail.

28
00:01:01,582 --> 00:01:03,068
Nobody wanted piles of dead

29
00:01:03,068 --> 00:01:04,730
rats at the police station.

30
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:08,846
It worked... on paper. At the peak,

31
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:10,680
over 20,000 tails

32
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,428
came in a single day.

33
00:01:12,678 --> 00:01:14,512
Then officials started seeing

34
00:01:14,512 --> 00:01:17,159
rats running around with no tails.

35
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:19,156
People had figured it out:

36
00:01:19,156 --> 00:01:21,444
cut off the tail, let the rat go,

37
00:01:21,444 --> 00:01:26,204
let it make more rats — and more tails.

38
00:01:26,204 --> 00:01:27,899
Inspectors even found rat

39
00:01:27,899 --> 00:01:29,546
farms outside the city.

40
00:01:29,796 --> 00:01:31,781
The city wanted fewer rats.

41
00:01:31,781 --> 00:01:34,997
It paid for tails. So it got tails.

42
00:01:34,997 --> 00:01:35,833
The hunt failed,

43
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:37,842
and the plague came anyway.

44
00:01:37,842 --> 00:01:39,572
Historian Michael Vann,

45
00:01:39,572 --> 00:01:40,698
who dug the story out

46
00:01:40,698 --> 00:01:41,917
of the colonial archives,

47
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:45,401
calls it "absolutely a disaster."

48
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:50,408
Ninety years later, in Britain,

49
00:01:50,408 --> 00:01:51,766
a company made the same mistake

50
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:55,613
with something far more expensive: airplane tickets.

51
00:01:55,863 --> 00:01:58,406
Hoover's British business was struggling.

52
00:01:58,406 --> 00:02:00,089
In August 1992,

53
00:02:00,089 --> 00:02:03,084
it made an offer to customers in Britain and Ireland:

54
00:02:03,084 --> 00:02:05,116
spend £100 on its products,

55
00:02:05,116 --> 00:02:08,367
get two free return flights to Europe.

56
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:10,621
Then it added New York and Orlando.

57
00:02:10,871 --> 00:02:15,492
The cheapest vacuum that qualified cost £119.

58
00:02:15,492 --> 00:02:17,373
The tickets were worth a lot more.

59
00:02:17,373 --> 00:02:18,777
People did the math.

60
00:02:18,777 --> 00:02:19,822
Stores sold out,

61
00:02:19,822 --> 00:02:24,176
and reportedly a market for barely-used vacuums appeared.

62
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:25,813
By March 1993,

63
00:02:25,813 --> 00:02:28,616
about 200,000 people had claimed flights.

64
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:30,933
Hoover wanted to sell vacuums.

65
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:33,394
It rewarded buying a vacuum.

66
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:37,051
But the reward was bigger than the thing it was attached to —

67
00:02:37,051 --> 00:02:39,628
so people weren't buying vacuums anymore.

68
00:02:39,628 --> 00:02:41,476
They were buying flights.

69
00:02:41,726 --> 00:02:43,862
Travel agents couldn't keep up.

70
00:02:43,862 --> 00:02:45,929
Customers waited, complained,

71
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:48,193
and one angry customer went all the way to Iowa

72
00:02:48,193 --> 00:02:51,130
to confront the owner at its shareholders' meeting.

73
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:54,091
Trading standards officers later found no fraud —

74
00:02:54,091 --> 00:02:56,575
just that Hoover had "grossly miscalculated"

75
00:02:56,575 --> 00:02:59,757
how many people would say yes.

76
00:02:59,757 --> 00:03:02,473
The owner, Maytag, paid for charter planes,

77
00:03:02,473 --> 00:03:04,354
removed three Hoover bosses,

78
00:03:04,354 --> 00:03:09,253
and took about $60 million in charges in 1993.

79
00:03:09,253 --> 00:03:12,295
Two years later, it sold Hoover Europe.

80
00:03:12,295 --> 00:03:15,872
Court cases over the flights dragged on until 1998.

81
00:03:19,017 --> 00:03:20,410
Now flip it around.

82
00:03:20,410 --> 00:03:21,641
What if the person on the other side

83
00:03:21,641 --> 00:03:23,780
reads the rules better than the company?

84
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:28,024
In 1999, Healthy Choice offered airline miles

85
00:03:28,024 --> 00:03:30,497
for barcodes from its products:

86
00:03:30,497 --> 00:03:32,946
500 miles for 10 barcodes,

87
00:03:32,946 --> 00:03:35,222
doubled if you mailed them early.

88
00:03:35,222 --> 00:03:37,254
Engineer David Phillips looked for

89
00:03:37,254 --> 00:03:39,924
the cheapest barcode in the store.

90
00:03:39,924 --> 00:03:41,619
Chocolate pudding.

91
00:03:41,619 --> 00:03:43,126
25 cents a cup.

92
00:03:43,376 --> 00:03:45,524
He bought over 12,000 cups,

93
00:03:45,524 --> 00:03:49,181
had Salvation Army volunteers peel the barcodes,

94
00:03:49,181 --> 00:03:51,967
and gave the pudding to people in need.

95
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:56,391
About $3,000 turned into over a million miles.

96
00:03:56,391 --> 00:03:59,050
The donation even got him a tax write-off,

97
00:03:59,050 --> 00:04:01,140
and Hollywood later borrowed his pudding plan

98
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:03,048
for an Adam Sandler movie.

99
00:04:03,298 --> 00:04:04,668
He didn't cheat.

100
00:04:04,668 --> 00:04:07,083
He did exactly what the rules paid for.

101
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:09,509
The company wanted people to buy its food.

102
00:04:09,509 --> 00:04:11,193
It paid for barcodes.

103
00:04:11,193 --> 00:04:13,747
And a barcode is just a barcode.

104
00:04:13,747 --> 00:04:15,488
Phillips later put it simply:

105
00:04:15,488 --> 00:04:17,056
"Companies still make mistakes

106
00:04:17,056 --> 00:04:18,805
like this all the time."

107
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:24,574
Rats, vacuums and pudding are funny.

108
00:04:24,574 --> 00:04:25,804
This one isn't.

109
00:04:26,054 --> 00:04:29,479
For years, one of America's biggest banks told everyone to

110
00:04:29,479 --> 00:04:31,162
watch one number:

111
00:04:31,162 --> 00:04:33,635
how many products each customer had.

112
00:04:33,635 --> 00:04:36,422
The CEO's goal was eight per customer.

113
00:04:36,422 --> 00:04:38,361
Senators later quoted his line:

114
00:04:38,361 --> 00:04:39,899
"eight is great."

115
00:04:40,149 --> 00:04:42,320
Employees had daily sales targets,

116
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,433
and bonuses tied to them.

117
00:04:44,433 --> 00:04:47,369
So some of them did the cheapest thing that made the number go up.

118
00:04:47,619 --> 00:04:50,510
They opened accounts customers never asked for.

119
00:04:50,510 --> 00:04:51,950
Checking accounts.

120
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:53,134
Savings accounts.

121
00:04:53,134 --> 00:04:55,386
Credit card applications.

122
00:04:55,386 --> 00:04:58,564
Sometimes with fees the customer then paid.

123
00:04:58,814 --> 00:05:06,337
In 2016, regulators fined Wells Fargo $185 million.

124
00:05:06,337 --> 00:05:08,044
The bank's own count:

125
00:05:08,044 --> 00:05:12,038
about 2 million accounts that may not have been authorized.

126
00:05:12,038 --> 00:05:16,771
A year later, a bigger review raised it to about 3.5 million.

127
00:05:17,021 --> 00:05:19,749
The regulator's director said it plainly:

128
00:05:19,749 --> 00:05:23,685
employees "secretly opened unauthorized accounts to hit sales

129
00:05:23,685 --> 00:05:26,286
targets and receive bonuses."

130
00:05:26,286 --> 00:05:30,094
Around 5,300 employees were fired.

131
00:05:30,094 --> 00:05:34,123
In 2020, the bank agreed to pay $3 billion to

132
00:05:34,123 --> 00:05:37,466
settle with the Justice Department and the SEC —

133
00:05:37,466 --> 00:05:40,253
which said the famous number investors watched had been

134
00:05:40,253 --> 00:05:41,727
inflated by unused,

135
00:05:41,727 --> 00:05:43,989
unneeded or unauthorized accounts.

136
00:05:44,239 --> 00:05:46,236
The bank wanted loyal customers.

137
00:05:46,236 --> 00:05:47,339
It paid for accounts.

138
00:05:47,339 --> 00:05:48,636
So it got accounts.

139
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,661
Sometimes it isn't a reward at all.

140
00:05:53,661 --> 00:05:56,546
It's a rule. Same trap.

141
00:05:56,796 --> 00:06:01,777
In November 1989, Mexico City tried to clean up its air with a simple rule:

142
00:06:01,777 --> 00:06:04,226
one weekday a week, your car stays home

143
00:06:04,226 --> 00:06:06,679
decided by the last digit of your license plate.

144
00:06:06,929 --> 00:06:10,751
The idea: take about one car in five off the road every weekday.

145
00:06:11,001 --> 00:06:13,137
But people still had to get to work.

146
00:06:13,137 --> 00:06:17,085
So some families bought a second car — with a different last digit.

147
00:06:17,085 --> 00:06:20,043
Often an older, cheaper one that polluted more.

148
00:06:20,293 --> 00:06:25,181
Economist Lucas Davis later studied years of readings from the city's air monitors.

149
00:06:25,181 --> 00:06:28,118
He found no evidence the rule made the air any cleaner

150
00:06:28,118 --> 00:06:30,596
and evidence that it put more cars on the road.

151
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:34,747
The city wanted cleaner air. It targeted license plates.

152
00:06:34,747 --> 00:06:37,871
So people bought license plates — with a car attached.

153
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:43,049
So is paying people always a mistake?

154
00:06:43,049 --> 00:06:45,562
Not at all. Here's when it worked.

155
00:06:45,812 --> 00:06:49,783
In 1932, wheat farmers in Western Australia

156
00:06:49,783 --> 00:06:53,103
many of them war veterans, already hit by the Depression

157
00:06:53,103 --> 00:06:57,364
watched around 20,000 emus eat their crops.

158
00:06:57,364 --> 00:07:00,162
The army sent soldiers with two machine guns.

159
00:07:00,162 --> 00:07:04,586
At a farm dam, over a thousand emus walked into a perfect ambush.

160
00:07:04,586 --> 00:07:07,372
The gun killed about a dozen... and jammed.

161
00:07:07,372 --> 00:07:10,867
They mounted a gun on a truck. The emus outran the truck.

162
00:07:10,867 --> 00:07:16,642
The Major reported 986 kills with 9,860 rounds — his own count.

163
00:07:16,798 --> 00:07:21,198
When farmers asked for soldiers again in 1934, the answer was no.

164
00:07:21,198 --> 00:07:24,124
What actually worked was older and cheaper:

165
00:07:24,124 --> 00:07:28,164
a cash bounty, paid to farmers for each emu.

166
00:07:28,164 --> 00:07:33,917
In six months of 1934, farmers filed over 57,000 bounty claims.

167
00:07:34,167 --> 00:07:35,769
The difference?

168
00:07:35,769 --> 00:07:41,644
The bounty paid for the thing the farmers actually wanted gone: the emu.

169
00:07:41,644 --> 00:07:46,462
Not a tail. Not a barcode. Not an account.

170
00:07:46,462 --> 00:07:48,017
The real result.

171
00:07:51,183 --> 00:07:53,157
Economists have a name for all of this.

172
00:07:53,157 --> 00:07:54,608
Goodhart's law:

173
00:07:54,608 --> 00:07:56,291
when a measure becomes a target,

174
00:07:56,291 --> 00:07:58,332
it stops being a good measure.

175
00:07:58,582 --> 00:08:00,800
So next time someone offers you a reward,

176
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,005
ask what it's really paying for.

177
00:08:03,005 --> 00:08:04,410
And if you're the one paying —

178
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:05,652
pay for the result.

179
00:08:05,652 --> 00:08:06,860
Not the number.
